1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,097
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,097 --> 00:00:07,819
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:07,819 --> 00:00:13,547
BlackRock, ticker BLK never reached the trigger, so no position was taken.

4
00:00:13,547 --> 00:00:16,953
The entry did its quiet job: it kept us out.

5
00:00:16,953 --> 00:00:21,889
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

6
00:00:21,889 --> 00:00:27,298
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

7
00:00:27,263 --> 00:00:30,732
On the day, the book is down 272 dollars.

8
00:00:30,732 --> 00:00:33,609
MetLife (MET) leads, +1.0 percent.

9
00:00:33,609 --> 00:00:36,147
Welltower (WELL) lags at -1.6.

10
00:00:36,147 --> 00:00:40,716
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

11
00:00:40,716 --> 00:00:44,243
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

12
00:00:44,199 --> 00:00:52,720
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

13
00:00:52,720 --> 00:01:00,788
12 positions, led by AbbVie, ticker ABBV, Eli Lilly and, ticker LLY, MetLife, ticker MET.

14
00:01:00,788 --> 00:01:13,117
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

15
00:01:13,084 --> 00:01:17,906
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

16
00:01:17,906 --> 00:01:20,317
Every morning OROS proposes.

17
00:01:20,317 --> 00:01:30,908
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

18
00:01:30,908 --> 00:01:34,869
Inside the book today: 5 positions up, 7 down.

19
00:01:34,869 --> 00:01:37,194
Best, DRH at +1.05 percent.

20
00:01:37,194 --> 00:01:39,002
Worst, ABNB at -1.84.

21
00:01:39,002 --> 00:01:43,997
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

22
00:01:43,997 --> 00:01:51,230
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -3.18% per trade over 1192 graded positions.

23
00:01:51,230 --> 00:01:58,290
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

24
00:01:58,290 --> 00:02:03,457
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

25
00:02:03,457 --> 00:02:15,856
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 166 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

26
00:02:15,824 --> 00:02:18,183
Over to the sports desk.

27
00:02:18,183 --> 00:02:27,914
Day 78 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

28
00:02:27,914 --> 00:02:34,499
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 104,151.

29
00:02:34,499 --> 00:02:40,299
Then the index fund, in gray, 1.0 thousand back at 103,159.

30
00:02:40,299 --> 00:02:47,867
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 7.1 thousand back at 97,100.

31
00:02:47,867 --> 00:02:50,619
The race has no finish line.

32
00:02:50,619 --> 00:02:55,141
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

33
00:02:55,141 --> 00:03:00,252
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

34
00:03:00,252 --> 00:03:02,217
Nothing is restated.

35
00:03:02,217 --> 00:03:11,125
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

36
00:03:11,087 --> 00:03:18,422
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

37
00:03:18,422 --> 00:03:20,663
Nothing here is financial advice.

38
00:03:20,663 --> 00:03:23,652
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
